WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.330
THE STATE OF AI:
In January of 2023, Microsoft announced that

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:09.050
they would be investing $10 billion into OpenAI,
the parent company of ChatGPT.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:14.740
It’s been over an year since that day and
OpenAI is now reporting that they’ve only

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.460
received a fraction of the promised $10 billion.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.490
This isn’t to say that Microsoft won't follow
through with their full investment.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:28.380
After all, $10 billion is just a drop in the
bucket for a $2.5 trillion giant like Microsoft.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:34.120
But, it’s also clear that Microsoft isn’t
exactly jumping up and down to pour more funding

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:35.920
into OpenAI.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.430
In fact, from the very beginning, Microsoft
structured the investment in tranches that

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:43.860
would need to be unlocked over a long period
of time.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:48.730
And reading between the lines, it seems that
tensions between OpenAI and Microsoft are

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.440
more than just financial.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:56.140
In November, for example, Microsoft was forced
to take a non voting position on the OpenAI

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.040
board despite owning 49% of the company.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:05.710
And with the whole Sam Altman fiasco, Microsoft
may even have legitimate reason to completely

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:10.430
back out of the deal, and that’s just the
most notable AI drama.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:14.330
It turns out that these sort of fallouts are
happening across the board throughout the

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:19.390
AI world as VCs pull back on funding AI projects
with ridiculous valuations.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:22.050
I mean, just think about this.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:27.610
OpenAI was looking to raise at a valuation
of $90 billion and the company barely pulls

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:30.500
in $1 billion in revenue per year.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:36.100
Not to mention, Sam Altman himself has admitted
that OpenAI will need a $100 billion not in

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.460
valuation, but in just funding to fully develop
generative intelligence.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:45.600
And all of this has been during a time in
which ChatGPT has been slowly bleeding users

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.360
month after month.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:53.070
None of this is to say that AI is not the
future because it obviously is, but it does

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:58.990
seem like the AI industry very much got ahead
of itself, and VCs are starting to pull the

00:01:58.990 --> 00:01:59.990
plug.

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:04.820
Just recently for example, a $4 billion AI
health startup named Olive, went under.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:09.490
And Olive is simply one of a growing list
of unicorns turned zombies.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:17.560
So, here’s the rise and fall of the AI hype
and what’s next for the AI industry.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:25.970
A TUMULTUOUS INDUSTRY:
All burgeoning industries go through boom

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:26.980
and bust cycles.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.980
That’s just how new innovations and tech
is created.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:35.760
You have these massive bursts of enthusiasm
and excitement followed by a harsh reality

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.260
check before true mainstream adoption begins.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:43.349
That’s exactly what happened with all the
dotcom companies in the early 2000s.

00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:48.310
After years of unlimited funding, the vast
majority of dotcom companies ended up going

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:51.040
under never to be heard of again.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.640
It was only after this cleansing that internet
companies like Amazon, Google, and Facebook

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.510
were really able to cement themselves for
the long term.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:04.250
In most industries, this sort of cycle takes
maybe 20 to 30 years, but the same cannot

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.250
be said about the AI industry.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:12.290
In fact, AI has been repeating this cycle
over and over again since the 1950s which

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:16.520
brings us back to the father of modern computing:
Alan Turing.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:22.170
In 1950, Turing would publish a landmark research
paper called “Computing Machinery and Intelligence”.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:27.640
In the paper, he explores the idea of whether
computers can think for which he offerd a

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.170
very simple answer.

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:33.730
If a computer is able to engage in conversation
with a human without the human noticing that

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:38.019
they’re talking to a computer, then, the
machine can indeed think.

00:03:38.019 --> 00:03:43.159
He dubbed this test the imitation game and
this is what would eventually become the famous

00:03:43.159 --> 00:03:44.390
Turing Test.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:49.299
Inspired by these ideas, hundreds of scientists
would begin working on their own AI projects

00:03:49.299 --> 00:03:51.740
hoping to pass the Turing Test.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:57.310
This included more simple applications like
a checkers bot in 1952 but it also included

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:58.620
generative AI.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:02.810
In fact, generative AI itself is by no means
a new invention.

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:07.750
As far back as the 1960s, we’ve seen scientists
grapple with the concept of language models

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:08.750
and chatbots.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:13.440
The ELIZA chatbot for example attempted to
simulate the work of a psychotherapist way

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.830
back in 1961.

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:19.500
Scientists were even working on the groundwork
for self driving vehicles.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:24.520
In the 1970s, for example, the Stanford cart
was able to navigate itself through room filled

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.120
with obstacles.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:29.180
It seemed like AI was truly the next big thing?

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:33.389
But, do you know what happened after these
breakthroughs?

00:04:33.389 --> 00:04:35.919
The first AI winter.

00:04:35.919 --> 00:04:40.759
Funding for AI startups was wiped out as investors
realized that it was a lot more profitable

00:04:40.759 --> 00:04:45.300
to invest in chip and computer companies with
strong revenue potential as opposed to these

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.090
theoretical projects.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:49.860
And that’s exactly what they did.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:54.590
After they made a crap ton of money with companies
like Sun Microsystems, Intel, Microsoft, Sony,

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:58.360
and Panasonic they would come back to the
speculation table.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:03.830
Japan, for example, would commit $850 million
to what they called the fifth generation computer

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:04.830
project.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:09.020
And IBM would begin work on next generation
AI called Deep Blue.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:13.550
The goal was to create a chess bot that was
better than the world had ever seen.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:19.300
This was by no means an easy task but in 1997,
IBM would do it.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:24.520
Deep Blue would decisively beat the chess
world champion at the time: Garry Kasparov.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:29.050
Once again, it seemed like AI was truly the
next big thing.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:34.110
Not only was AI able to outsmart an average
joe but a world champion.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:38.130
This was definitive proof that AI could do
great things.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:41.889
But, after that, we would enter yet another
AI winter.

00:05:41.889 --> 00:05:46.140
With investors losing money hand over fist
during the dotcom bust, the last thing they

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:50.840
wanted to do was speculate on AI projects
with no revenue potential.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:55.520
That was until they got rich again thanks
to web companies like Google, Facebook, Amazon,

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.710
Uber, AirBnB and so on bringing us into the
current cycle.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:06.750
As you can see, for 70 years now, AI has consistently
been this sort of elusive next big thing that’s

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.560
almost within grasp but not quite.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.039
So, will this time be any different?

00:06:12.039 --> 00:06:15.099
Well, at some point, it does have to be different.

00:06:15.099 --> 00:06:18.090
That’s kind of how AI eventually becomes
mainstream.

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:23.310
But, judging by history, there’s no guarantee
that this cycle is the cycle.

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:27.960
That’s what people have been thinking for
the past 70 years only to be proven wrong

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:33.560
over and over again which brings us into the
question of why?

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:42.479
THE AI PITFALLS:
One of the biggest themes surrounding each

00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:46.270
and every one of these AI winters is funding.

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:51.120
The reality is that AI is an extremely expensive
game, and it’s only getting more and more

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.060
expensive each cycle.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:58.360
OpenAI for example is having to pay $1 million
per year to get access to the right engineers.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:02.770
Not to mention the massive data centers and
loads of GPUs they need to make the entire

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:04.280
operation possible.

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.860
This is why Sam Altman thinks they need $100
billion.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:13.860
But, all of that is only half the story because
AI is a doubled edged sword.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:19.169
Not only does it take a stupid amount of money
to bring to market but it usually has very

00:07:19.169 --> 00:07:22.550
little revenue potential atleast in its original
state.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:26.880
It often has be evolved substantially before
being able to generate serious revenue.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:30.960
As such, investors are only willing to take
this gamble when times are good.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.001
When their other investments are doing well,
when the tech industry as a whole is booming,

00:07:35.001 --> 00:07:38.860
and when they don’t know where else to invest
their money.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:43.110
This was exactly what was happening throughout
the early 2020s.

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:47.860
Investors had hit it big with web companies
and unicorns, and with 0% interest rates,

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:52.860
they simply did not know where to put all
those profits, so they put it into AI.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:58.310
But when the music stops playing as was the
case in the 1970s, the early 2000s, and right

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:01.970
now, they’re quick to cut losses and move
back to safety.

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:07.220
But, even putting the whole financing of AI
aside, it seems that the industry is always

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.440
stopped by a consistent internal factors themselves.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.300
For example, limited computing power.

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:17.520
Even in the modern day where we have enough
computing power for basically any task imaginable,

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:21.990
somehow, we don’t have enough computing
power for AI.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:26.949
And the main reason for this is that our needs
for AI are also constantly evolving.

00:08:26.949 --> 00:08:31.199
Before we were just trying to create AIs that
could hold conversation or excel at a board

00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:32.199
game.

00:08:32.199 --> 00:08:36.419
Today, we’re trying to create AIs that can
not only hold conversation but scour the entire

00:08:36.419 --> 00:08:38.580
web to answer our questions.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:43.180
Similarly, we’re no longer trying to create
carts that can navigate a room within 5 hours

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:46.760
but cars that can navigate any road live.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:51.550
Our demands of AI are rising exponentially
and as such, the resources needed to make

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:55.260
these projects a reality are also rising exponentially.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:09:00.310
But even regardless of how many resources
we have, it’s usually only a matter of time

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:05.240
until we run into Moravec’s Paradox which
describes the odd disparity between human

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:07.590
and computer intellgience.

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:11.820
It turns out that doing things that are super
hard for humans like mass data analysis and

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:14.870
calculations are super easy for computers.

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:19.780
However, doing things that are easy for humans
like sensing depth, fine motor skills, and

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:23.829
innately leveraging feedback control systems
are near impossible.

00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:28.890
And if all of those challenges were not hard
enough to overcome themselves, well, we have

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:32.360
yet to discuss the biggest challenge which
is the rise of AI phonies.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:44.190
THE AI PHONIES:
With any hot trend, it’s only a matter of

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:47.660
time until the opportunists step into the
scene.

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:53.570
In crypto for example, for every bitcoin and
ethereum, there’s 100s of trash coins, rug

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:56.310
pulls, NFT projects, and straight up scams.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:10:01.860
It’s the same thing with the AI space, just
in a more sophisticated manner.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:08.260
For every OpenAI or Tesla FSD type effort,
you’ve got dozens garbage startups.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:13.730
Most of them have nothing to do with AI whatsoever
or they barely leverage AI, but they’ll

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:18.180
put it all over their marketing and brand
themselves as an AI company.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:21.740
In reality, they’re just a construction
or consulting firm.

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:27.170
But all of a sudden, they're an AI based construction
firm or an AI based consulting firm.

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:32.170
From an outside perspective, these are obviously
trash companies that way overstating their

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:33.800
use of AI.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:39.470
But for desperate VCs who missed out on investing
in OpenAI, this truly seems like the future.

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:45.019
Sure, they might’ve missed out on bringing
AI to search or cars, but they can still be

00:10:45.019 --> 00:10:50.380
the ones to bring it to the sewer industry
or the energy industry even though the application

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:52.260
is far more limited.

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:57.610
Fortuantely, this BS doesn’t go one forever,
but the fall of these zombie companies is

00:10:57.610 --> 00:10:59.240
by no means graceful.

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:04.990
Usually, what happens is investors pull back
on all kinds of AI investing and only the

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:10.010
good companies end up surviving the funding
cut which is exactly what’s happening right

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:11.250
now.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:17.140
Global VC funding has plummeted from $170
billion in the 4th quarter of 2021 to $70

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:20.330
billion in the 3rd quarter of 2023.

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:25.190
AI was one of the few sectors that was holding
up through this funding collapse, but we’re

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:28.000
starting to see cracks within the AI world
as well.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:34.560
In Q3 of 2023, for example, the number of
AI VC deals shrunk by 27%.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:41.670
Technically, the total value of these deals
increased by 39% to $6.1 billion, but $4 billion

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:45.800
of that was just due to one investment from
Amazon.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:49.620
And you could say the same thing about all
of 2023 actually.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:54.410
The main reason that the AI industry has held
up as well as it did was simply due to a handful

00:11:54.410 --> 00:12:00.540
of massive deals from companies like Microsoft,
Nvidia, Google, Salesforce, and Amazon.

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:05.050
When we take these flagship deals out of the
picture though, the outlook for rest of the

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:10.800
AI industry becomes quite bleak, and that
brings us into the question of what happens

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:11.800
next?

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:16.070
Well, most likely another AI winter filled
with bankruptcies.

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:21.709
The flagship companies like OpenAI will most
likely survive but you probably won't hear

00:12:21.709 --> 00:12:25.580
nearly as much about them on the news as much
as they enter a new phase of research and

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:27.950
development away from the public light.

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:31.230
That isn’t to say that this cycle was useless
though.

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:34.920
Another intrinsically cyclical sector is the
chip industry.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:39.750
They have booms and busts basically every
decade or even multiple times per decade.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:44.449
But, just because the industry is volatile
doesn't mean that they’re not making forward

00:12:44.449 --> 00:12:45.910
progress.

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:50.620
Chips today are not even comparable to chips
from the 1950s, and it’s the same thing

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:51.680
with AI.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:56.530
Historically, it’s simply been a highly
volatile industry, and we’re currently going

00:12:56.530 --> 00:13:03.339
from a high to low, but eventually, the industry
will see the other side once again.

00:13:03.339 --> 00:13:05.480
Do you think AI is overhyped?

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:07.080
Comment that down below.

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:11.250
Another industry that’s going through a
massive reality check is big tech, check out

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:15.500
this video to learn more.

